🚽 Test case: regression -- the struct-field counterpart of char_array_
🚽 write_order.brainrot. write_value_to_address()'s VAR_CHAR case wrote
🚽 the full 4-byte int width even when the address was a packed struct
🚽 field, not a scalar variable's union slot. `b` and `c` below are
🚽 adjacent 1-byte fields (offsets 4 and 5 in a `{ rizz a; yap b; yap c;
🚽 }` layout); writing `c` before `b` used to have `b`'s wrongly-widened
🚽 write silently zero the 3 bytes after it -- which is `c`'s own byte.
🚽
🚽 Reads use `+ 0` (forcing NODE_STRUCT_ACCESS through evaluate_
🚽 expression_int's integer-family path), not `%c`: `%c` only keeps the
🚽 low 8 bits, so it can't tell a correct 1-byte read from a stale 4-byte
🚽 one whose low byte happens to still be right. Values are >= 128, not
🚽 ASCII, so this also catches a second regression a `%c`/ASCII-only
🚽 fixture can't: NODE_STRUCT_ACCESS's VAR_CHAR read must be `(int)*
🚽 (unsigned char *)addr`, not `(int)*(char *)addr` -- plain `char` is
🚽 signed on this platform, so a signed read of a byte >= 128 disagrees
🚽 with char_scalar_slot_value()'s always-0-255 scalar representation
🚽 (confirmed: before this fix, a signed struct-field read of 200 gave
🚽 -56, not 200).
gang Foo {
    rizz a;
    yap b;
    yap c;
};

skibidi main {
    gang Foo f;
    f.a = 1;
    f.c = 200;
    f.b = 150;
    yapping("%d %d %d", f.a, f.b + 0, f.c + 0);
}
